It has been a summer of change for women=E2=80=99s football in England, wit=
h expansion of the Women=E2=80=99s Super League to 14 teams bringing three =
new sides into the fold, huge transfers hitting the headlines, changes to t=
he League Cup and a new name on the WSL trophy as we prepare for kick-off o=
n Friday.

Players have had a proper break over the summer, with no major internationa=
l women=E2=80=99s tournament for the first time in six years (Covid delays =
pushed back the Tokyo Olympics to 2021 and the European Championship to 202=
2, followed by the 2023 World Cup, 2024 Paris Olympics and Euro 2025). As a=
 result, managers have had a full pre-season with all available players for=
 the first time in more than half a decade too.


