olor:#121212;"><p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">After Don Revie had walked =
out on the England job to become manager of the UAE, England turned to the =
former West Ham manager Ron Greenwood to replace him. He had been a moderat=
e defender, playing for Bradford Park Avenue, Brentford, Chelsea and Fulham=
, winning one England B cap, but had made his reputation as a coach after t=
he future chairman of the Football Association, Sir Harold Thompson, saw hi=
m working with the Oxford University side. After stints with England=E2=80=
=99s Under-23s and as assistant to George Swindin with Arsenal, he took cha=
rge of West Ham, leading them to FA Cup and Cup-Winners=E2=80=99 Cup glory.=
 His first game in charge of England, initially as a caretaker, came on 7 S=
eptember 1977, as England faced Switzerland in a friendly at Wembley. He pi=
cked five Liverpool players, including Ian Callaghan, who had previously be=
en capped 11 years earlier, and Kevin Keegan, who had left Liverpool that s=
ummer, on the logic that the England team should be based around the strong=
est club side in the country at the time. A 0-0 draw, though, highlighted c=
reative problems that would dog his reign. Although England won their two r=
emaining World Cup qualifiers, they missed out on the 1978 tournament on go=
al-difference, and they went out of the 1982 World Cup, Greenwood=E2=80=99s=
 final tournament, after a pair of 0-0 draws.</p></div></td></tr>
